\name{proteins_classify_peptides}
\alias{proteins_classify_peptides}
\title{Classify peptides by how widely they are shared across protein databases, with I = L}
\usage{
proteins_classify_peptides(peptides, databases, contaminants = NULL, threads = 1,
  timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{peptides}{A character vector of unmodified base sequences, upper case (\code{"PEPTIDEK"}), one
  result row each in the same order, duplicates included. Sent on stdin. Strip modifications
  first: mzLib refuses a sequence with a bracketed modification, or in lower case
  (\code{"peptidek"}), rather than guess.}

\item{databases}{The search database or databases: UniProt XML or FASTA, optionally gzipped.}

\item{contaminants}{Contaminant databases, searched alongside: they are real sequences in the
  search space, so a peptide a contaminant shares with a target is shared.}

\item{threads}{Databases read at once (the classification itself is one pass). Default 1; \code{-1}
  means every core. Same answer at any value.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} (the default) to wait.
}

}
\description{
Classify peptides as unique to one sequence, shared within a gene, shared across genes, or not in the databases, treating I and L as the same residue.

Wraps mzLib's \code{PeptideUniquenessClassifier.Classify}. Each peptide is one of:
}
\details{
\code{"Unique"} - every protein containing it has the \strong{same sequence} (identical entries under two
accessions count as one; both are listed). \code{"SharedWithinGene"} - several distinct sequences, all
sharing a gene: isoforms, so the peptide supports the gene, not an isoform.
\code{"SharedAcrossGenes"} - sequences with no gene in common. \code{"NotInDatabase"} - no target protein
contains it.

A protein contains a peptide when its sequence contains it \strong{anywhere, whatever the protease} -
deliberately conservative, so a peptide called \code{"Unique"} cannot be explained by another entry
at a site the search's cleavage rules happened to skip. \strong{I and L are the same residue}
throughout, because a mass spectrometer cannot tell them apart.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_peptide_classification}. \code{file_count} counts the database files searched;
  \code{peptide_count} counts the peptides (rows); \code{target_protein_count} counts the proteins
  searched, contaminants included; \code{decoy_proteins_ignored} counts the decoy proteins, never
  searched. \code{sharing_counts} is a named vector: class to number of peptides, every class
  present. \code{i_and_l_equivalent} is always \code{TRUE}, carried so the rule travels with the result.

  \code{peptides} is a data.frame with one row per peptide, in the order given: \code{peptide} (exactly as
  given, not I/L-folded), \code{sharing}, \code{accession_count} (the target proteins containing it), and
  the list columns \code{accessions} and \code{shared_gene_keys} (the gene keys common to all of them, such
  as \code{"ensembl:ENSG00000111640"}; empty for \code{"NotInDatabase"} and \code{"SharedAcrossGenes"}).

}
\section{Why there is no on_error}{


Every database is part of one search space. Skipping one that failed to load would report the
peptides it contains as unique, so any failure raises.

}
